  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1222 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nThe long term forecast will start off with a cooler and cloudy \nweekend. The cloud deck that is expected to fill in across the \nforecast area Friday is expected to prevail Saturday and Sunday \nkeeping high temperatures in the 60s. Precipitation chances also \nreturn this weekend with an upper pattern change. An upper low will \ndevelop across the Desert Southwest before swinging across northern \nMexico. This upper pattern change will shift the flow aloft to the \nsouthwest, ushering in subtropical moisture to the mid-to-upper \nlevels. The influx of subtropical moisture in combination with the \nmoisture behind the front will bring chances for rain showers over \nthe weekend. Showers are expected to begin overnight Friday into \nearly Saturday across western portions of the forecast area before \nexpanding east. Showers will then clear out to the south and east \nthrough late Sunday and early Monday. Not much is expected from \nthese showers besides light to moderate rain with storm total QPF \naround 0.5 inches or less across the region with higher amounts \nacross the Northern Edwards Plateau and Northwest Hill Country \nregions. As the upper low swings across Texas and continues to push \neast next week, upper ridging will begin to amplify up the Rockies. \nAs the ridging amplifies and pushes east, temperatures will warm \nback up to the 70s and conditions will dry out.",
